To fix the CORS error in your Next.js application when using WordPress as the backend, you need to add the necessary CORS headers to your WordPress server. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Install and activate the 'WP CORS' plugin on your WordPress site. This plugin allows you to easily manage CORS settings for your API.
2. Go to the 'Settings' > 'WP CORS' page in your WordPress dashboard.
3. Click on the 'Add New Policy' button to create a new CORS policy.
4. In the 'Policy Name' field, enter a name for your policy (e.g., 'Next.js Frontend').
5. In the 'Allowed Origins' field, enter the URL of your Next.js frontend (e.g., `http://localhost:3000`). You can also use a wildcard (*) to allow requests from any origin.
6. In the 'Allowed Methods' field, select the HTTP methods you want to allow (e.g., GET, POST, PUT, DELETE). You can also select 'Any' to allow all methods.
7. In the 'Allowed Headers' field, select the headers you want to allow (e.g., 'Authorization', 'Content-Type'). You can also select 'Any' to allow all headers.
8. Click on the 'Save Policy' button to save your policy.
9. Now, you need to add the CORS header to your WordPress API endpoints. You can do this by adding the following code to your `functions.php` file or a site-specific plugin:
```php
function add_cors_headers($headers) {
    $headers['Access-Control-Allow-Origin'] = get_option('wp_cors_allowed_origins');
    $headers['Access-Control-Allow-Methods'] = get_option('wp_cors_allowed_methods');
    $headers['Access-Control-Allow-Headers'] = get_option('wp_cors_allowed_headers');
    return $headers;
}
add_filter('rest_api_init', 'add_cors_headers');
```
This code will add the CORS headers to your WordPress API endpoints, allowing your Next.js frontend to make requests to your API

