Here's an example of a generic REST service wrapper class in C# with auth token handling:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

public class RestServiceWrapper<T>
{
    private readonly string _baseUrl;
    private readonly string _authToken;

    public RestServiceWrapper(string baseUrl, string authToken)
    {
        _baseUrl = baseUrl;
        _authToken = authToken;
    }

    public async Task<T> GetAsync()
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", _authToken);
            var response = await client.GetAsync(_baseUrl);
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
            return await response.Content.ReadAsAsync<T>();
        }
    }
}

public class MyClass
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
}

public class Program
{
    public static async Task Main(string[] args)
    {
        var restServiceWrapper = new RestServiceWrapper<MyClass>("https://example.com/api/my-class", "abc123");
        var result = await restServiceWrapper.GetAsync();
        Console.WriteLine(result);
    }
}
```

In this example, the `RestServiceWrapper` class takes a base URL and an authentication token as constructor parameters. The `GetAsync` method makes a GET request to the specified URL, including the authentication token in the Authorization header. The method returns the response data as a deserialized object of type `T`.

The `MyClass` class is a simple example of a data model that can be used with the `RestServiceWrapper` class. The `Program` class demonstrates how to use the `RestServiceWrapper` class to fetch data from a REST API.

