Here's a simple decorator for Nest.js middleware that checks the `NODE_ENV` variable and calls `next()` if it's equal to 'true', but returns `false` if it's equal to 'false':

```typescript
import { Injectable, CanActivate, ExecutionContext } from '@nestjs/common';

@Injectable()
export class EnvironmentMiddleware implements CanActivate {
  canActivate(context: ExecutionContext): boolean {
    const request = context.switchToHttp().getRequest();
    const nodeEnv = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    if (nodeEnv === 'true') {
      return true;
    } else if (nodeEnv === 'false') {
      return false;
    }

    return true; // Default behavior is to allow the request
  }
}
```

To use this middleware, you can add it to your module's `imports` array:

```typescript
import { Module } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvironmentMiddleware } from './environment.middleware';

@Module({
  imports: [EnvironmentMiddleware],
})
export class AppModule {}
```

Then, in your controller or service, you can inject the `EnvironmentMiddleware` and use it to protect your endpoints:

```typescript
import { Controller, Get } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvironmentMiddleware } from './environment.middleware';

@Controller('api/protected')
export class ProtectedController {
  constructor(private readonly environmentMiddleware: EnvironmentMiddleware) {}

  @Get()
  async getProtectedData() {
    await this.environmentMiddleware.canActivate(this);

    // Your protected data logic here
  }
}
```

This way, only requests with `NODE_ENV=true` will be allowed to access the `getProtectedData` endpoint. Requests with `NODE_ENV=false` will be returned `false`.

