To prevent the planets from being too close together, you can implement a simple collision detection system and adjust their positions accordingly. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function that includes this logic:

```javascript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    } else {
      // Adjust the planet's position to avoid collision
      const offset = randomPosition(-minDistance, minDistance);
      planet.position = planet.position.map((pos, index) => pos + offset);
    }
  }

  return planets;
};
```

This function will now randomly generate planets until it has created the desired number of planets without any collisions. The `offset` variable is used to adjust the planet's position if it's too close to another planet. You can adjust the `minDistance` variable to control the minimum distance between the planets.

Also, make sure to call the `generateRandomPlanets` function inside the `useEffect` hook to update the `planets` state when the component mounts or when the number of planets changes.

