Here's a PowerShell script that recursively checks folders to see if their ACLs refer to SIDs which occur only in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain:

```powershell
# Define the function to check if an SID exists only in the SIDHistory
function CheckSIDInSIDHistory {
    param($sid)
    $domain = (Get-ADDomain -Filter *).Name
    $searcher = New-Object System.DirectoryServices.DirectorySearcher($domain)
    $searcher.Filter = "(&(objectClass=user)(sid=$sid))"
    $results = $searcher.FindAll()
    return ($results.Count -eq 0)
}

# Define the function to recursively check folders
function CheckFolders {
    param($path)
    Get-ChildItem -Path $path -Directory | ForEach-Object {
        $acl = Get-Acl -Path $_.FullName
        $sidHistory = @()
        foreach ($access in $acl.Access) {
            if ($access.IdentityReference -match '^S-1-5-21-') {
                $sid = New-Object System.Security.Principal.SecurityIdentifier($access.IdentityReference)
                if (CheckSIDInSIDHistory($sid.Value)) {
                    $sidHistory += $sid.Value
                }
            }
        }
        if ($sidHistory.Count -gt 0) {
            Write-Host "Folder '$($_.Name)' has ACLs referring to SIDs only in the SIDHistory:"
            Write-Host $sidHistory -ForegroundColor Yellow
        }
        CheckFolders($_.FullName)
    }
}

# Call the function with the root path
CheckFolders("C:\")
```

This script will recursively check all folders in the C:\ drive and its subdirectories for ACLs that refer to SIDs only in the SIDHistory of any user or group in the domain. Any folders with such ACLs will be displayed with the corresponding SIDs highlighted in yellow.
